msgid "Azure Functions with Quarkus REST, Undertow, or Reactive Routes"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `quarkus-azure-functions-http` extension allows you to write "
"microservices with Quarkus REST (our Jakarta REST implementation),\n"
"Undertow (servlet), Reactive Routes, or xref:funqy-http.adoc[Funqy HTTP] and "
"make these microservices deployable to the Azure Functions runtime.\n"
"In other words, this extension is a bridge from the Azure Functions "
"HttpTrigger and the Quarkus family\n"
"of HTTP APIs.\n"
"One azure function deployment can represent any number of Jakarta REST, "
"servlet, Reactive Routes, or xref:funqy-http.adoc[Funqy HTTP] endpoints."
msgstr ""

msgid "This technology is considered {extension-status}."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In _preview_, backward compatibility and presence in the ecosystem is not "
"guaranteed.\n"
"Specific improvements might require changing configuration or APIs, and "
"plans to become _stable_ are under way.\n"
"Feedback is welcome on our https://groups.google.com/d/forum/quarkus-"
"dev[mailing list] or as issues in our https://github.com/quarkusio/quarkus/"
"issues[GitHub issue tracker]."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For a full list of possible statuses, check our https://quarkus.io/faq/#what-"
"are-the-extension-statuses[FAQ entry]."
msgstr ""

msgid "Prerequisites"
msgstr ""

msgid "To complete this guide, you need:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Roughly 15 minutes"
msgstr ""

msgid "An IDE"
msgstr ""

msgid "JDK 17+ installed with `JAVA_HOME` configured appropriately"
msgstr ""

msgid "Apache Maven {maven-version}"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Optionally the xref:cli-tooling.adoc[Quarkus CLI] if you want to use it"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Optionally Mandrel or GraalVM installed and xref:building-native-image."
"adoc#configuring-graalvm[configured appropriately] if you want to build a "
"native executable (or Docker if you use a native container build)"
msgstr ""

msgid "https://azure.microsoft.com[An Azure Account].  Free accounts work."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/functions-run-"
"local#v2[Azure Functions Core Tools] version 4.x"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/cli/azure/install-azure-cli[Azure CLI "
"Installed]"
msgstr ""

msgid "Solution"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This guide walks you through running a maven project that can deploy a "
"Quarkus REST endpoint to Azure Functions.\n"
"While only Jakarta REST is provided as an example, you can easily replace it "
"with the HTTP framework of your choice."
msgstr ""

msgid "Creating the Maven/Gradle Project"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"First, we need a new project. Create a new project with the following "
"command:"
msgstr ""

msgid "CLI"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To create a Gradle project, add the `--gradle` or `--gradle-kotlin-dsl` "
"option."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For more information about how to install and use the Quarkus CLI, see the "
"xref:cli-tooling.adoc[Quarkus CLI] guide."
msgstr ""

msgid "Maven"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To create a Gradle project, add the `-DbuildTool=gradle` or `-DbuildTool="
"gradle-kotlin-dsl` option."
msgstr ""

msgid "For Windows users:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If using cmd, (don't use backward slash `\\` and put everything on the same "
"line)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If using Powershell, wrap `-D` parameters in double quotes e.g. `\"-"
"DprojectArtifactId={create-app-artifact-id}\"`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This command generates a project structure importing the Quarkus Azure "
"Functions HTTP extension.\n"
"It includes an example."
msgstr ""

msgid "Examining the project"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you open the `pom.xml` or `build.gradle` build file of the generated "
"project you'll see that\n"
"the project is similar to any other Quarkus project.\n"
"The `quarkus-azure-functions-http` extension is the integration point "
"between\n"
"Quarkus and Azure Functions."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The current implementation of the `quarkus-azure-functions-http` extension "
"no longer requires the\n"
"`azure-functions-maven-plugin` or Gradle equivalent.  Local development and "
"Azure Functions packaging and\n"
"deployment is now all done by Quarkus."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Build configuration is all within `application.properties`. The only "
"required configuration switch\n"
"is `quarkus.azure-functions.app-name`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Azure Deployment Descriptors"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Azure Functions `host.json` deployment descriptor is automatically\n"
"generated, but if you need to override it, declare it in the root directory "
"of the project and\n"
"rerun the build when you are ready."
msgstr ""

msgid "Configuring Root Paths"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The default route prefix for an Azure Function is `/api`.  All of your "
"Jakarta REST, Servlet, Reactive Routes, and xref:funqy-http.adoc[Funqy HTTP] "
"endpoints must\n"
"explicitly take this into account.  In the generated project, this is "
"handled by the\n"
"`quarkus.http.root-path` switch in `application.properties`"
msgstr ""

msgid "Quarkus dev mode"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus dev mode works by just running your application as an HTTP endpoint. "
" In dev mode,\n"
"there is no special interaction with the Azure Functions local runtime."
msgstr ""

msgid "Run locally in Azure Functions local environment"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you want to try this example within the local Azure Functions "
"environment, you can\n"
"use this command:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Gradle is a bit quirky with process management, so you need the `--no-"
"daemon` switch or control-c will not\n"
"destroy the process cleanly and you'll have open ports."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You must have the https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/"
"functions-run-local#v2[Azure Functions Core Tools]\n"
"installed for this to work."
msgstr ""

msgid "The URL to access the example would be:"
msgstr ""

msgid "http://localhost:8081/api/hello"
msgstr ""

msgid "Quarkus Integration Testing"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can implement integration tests using `@QuarkusIntegrationTest` "
"functionality.  When these\n"
"integration tests run, the local Azure Functions environment will be spun up "
"for the duration of integration testing."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Make sure any integration tests you execute with Maven use the `*IT.java` "
"file pattern so that regular builds do not execute\n"
"the test."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Make sure any integration tests you execute with Gradle are located within "
"`src/integrationTest/java`. Integration\n"
"tests that exist in `src/test` will run with normal build and fail."
msgstr ""

msgid "Login to Azure"
msgstr ""

msgid "To be able to deploy your application to Azure, log in to Azure:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Deploy to Azure"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `quarkus-azure-functions-http` extension handles all the work to deploy "
"to Azure. By default,\n"
"Quarkus will use the Azure CLI in the background to authenticate and deploy "
"to Azure. If you have\n"
"multiple subscriptions associated with your account, you must set the "
"`quarkus.azure-functions.subscription-id`\n"
"property in your `application.properties` file to the subscription you want "
"to use.\n"
"For other authentication mechanisms and deployment options, see "
"<<#configuration-reference>>."
msgstr ""

msgid "To deploy your application, after you built your project, execute:"
msgstr ""

msgid "For Gradle, you must use the `--info` switch to see this output."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If deployment is a success, Quarkus will output the endpoint URL of the "
"example function to the console:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The URL to access the service would be something like: `https://{appName}."
"azurewebsites.net/api/hello`."
msgstr ""

